WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.359
You change one setting, you apply it, and there is no error.

2
00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:08.777
Yet the app keeps showing the old value.

3
00:00:08.777 --> 00:00:09.985
Did something break?

4
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.146
Let us start in the restaurant.

5
00:00:12.146 --> 00:00:15.782
At noon, the manager briefs the waiters on the specials.

6
00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:17.146
Today's soup: tomato.

7
00:00:17.146 --> 00:00:19.419
That is on the board in the office.

8
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.215
At two, the manager changes the soup to pumpkin, and writes it on the board.

9
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.254
But the waiters heard the specials once, at the start of their shift.

10
00:00:28.254 --> 00:00:32.499
So they keep offering tomato soup, and the kitchen keeps saying it has none.

11
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:34.170
Nobody did anything wrong.

12
00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.087
The news reached the board, but not the people who had already heard the old version.

13
00:00:39.087 --> 00:00:41.689
Your app can have exactly the same afternoon.

14
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:52.467
Since the Docker lessons, we have built one image, and changed how it behaves with environment variables, named settings a program reads when it starts.

15
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:54.480
The same image runs everywhere.

16
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.103
Only the settings differ.

17
00:00:56.267 --> 00:01:00.318
In Kubernetes, most teams keep those settings in a ConfigMap.

18
00:01:00.318 --> 00:01:03.041
A small object with keys and text values.

19
00:01:03.041 --> 00:01:05.033
It is the board in the office.

20
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.788
A container can read it in two main ways.

21
00:01:07.788 --> 00:01:11.322
With envFrom, every key becomes an environment variable.

22
00:01:11.322 --> 00:01:14.162
That is the briefing at the start of a shift.

23
00:01:14.162 --> 00:01:16.750
Or as a volume: every key becomes a file.

24
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:21.041
That is a board on the kitchen wall, which anyone can look at again.

25
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:24.626
Let us shrink this to the lesson's demo.

26
00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:28.261
One ConfigMap, demo-config, with one key: COLOR, blue.

27
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.114
And one Pod that reads that value twice.

28
00:01:31.114 --> 00:01:34.643
Once as an environment variable, and once as a file.

29
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.490
Every five seconds, it prints both.

30
00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.104
env equals blue, file equals blue.

31
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:43.350
Now change the value in the ConfigMap to green.

32
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.400
Pause and guess.

33
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.303
What will the next lines say?

34
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.439
For a while, nothing changes at all.

35
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.983
Then, on the kubelet's next sync, usually within a minute or so, the file shows green.

36
00:01:57.133 --> 00:01:59.113
And the environment variable?

37
00:01:59.113 --> 00:01:59.523
Never.

38
00:01:59.523 --> 00:02:02.937
It stays blue for the whole life of the container.

39
00:02:02.937 --> 00:02:04.371
The briefing is over.

40
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:05.121
Now wait.

41
00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:08.973
Didn't we learn that Kubernetes runs a loop for everything?

42
00:02:08.973 --> 00:02:13.087
Surely some controller notices the change and restarts the Pod?

43
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:13.482
No.

44
00:02:13.482 --> 00:02:15.278
A ConfigMap is only data.

45
00:02:15.278 --> 00:02:17.074
No controller acts on it.

46
00:02:17.074 --> 00:02:23.826
The Deployment controller watches its own Pod template, not the ConfigMaps the template names.

47
00:02:23.826 --> 00:02:27.273
So changing a ConfigMap never restarts anything.

48
00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:31.262
The kubelet, the helper on each node, reads it at two moments.

49
00:02:31.262 --> 00:02:34.997
When it starts a container, to fill in environment variables.

50
00:02:34.997 --> 00:02:38.303
And on its regular sync, to refresh the mounted files.

51
00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:41.828
How does the kubelet swap a file safely?

52
00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:42.932
List the folder.

53
00:02:42.932 --> 00:02:46.522
The real data sits in a folder named after the time.

54
00:02:46.522 --> 00:02:49.973
The name dot dot data is a link, a pointer, to it.

55
00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:54.953
On a change, the kubelet writes a new folder, and moves the link in one step.

56
00:02:54.953 --> 00:02:57.144
So the app never reads half a file.

57
00:02:57.333 --> 00:03:05.046
That link trick is also why a file mounted with subPath, one file placed into an existing folder, is never updated.

58
00:03:05.046 --> 00:03:09.272
And even an updated file only helps if your app reads it again.

59
00:03:09.272 --> 00:03:11.753
Most apps read config once, at start.

60
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:15.646
So how do the waiters hear about the pumpkin soup?

61
00:03:15.646 --> 00:03:16.394
A new shift.

62
00:03:16.394 --> 00:03:18.697
The new waiters get a fresh briefing.

63
00:03:18.867 --> 00:03:24.971
Our demo is a bare Pod, so we replace it: delete it, and create it again from the same spec.

64
00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:27.890
The new container starts with the new value.

65
00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.279
env equals green, file equals green.

66
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.974
hello-devops runs as a Deployment, and it reads GREETING through envFrom.

67
00:03:35.974 --> 00:03:38.133
So a new greeting needs new Pods.

68
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:41.076
The tool for that is kubectl rollout restart.

69
00:03:41.667 --> 00:03:46.431
It writes the current time into an annotation, a note, in the Pod template.

70
00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:54.702
The template changed, so the Deployment does a normal rolling update, and every new Pod starts with the new values.

71
00:03:54.702 --> 00:03:56.544
A new shift, freshly briefed.

72
00:03:56.733 --> 00:03:58.477
Tools have a neater trick.

73
00:03:58.477 --> 00:04:05.853
Kustomize's configMapGenerator adds a hash, a short code worked out from the content, to the ConfigMap's name.

74
00:04:05.853 --> 00:04:11.687
New content means a new name, so the template changes, and a rollout happens by itself.

75
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.320
One guardrail.

76
00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.709
A Deployment rollback brings back the old Pod template, but not the old ConfigMap.

77
00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:23.177
Change both, roll back, and the old code runs with the new settings.

78
00:04:23.177 --> 00:04:27.317
Like reprinting last week's menu with this week's prices on it.

79
00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:30.111
The fix is to treat config like images.

80
00:04:30.111 --> 00:04:34.654
Never change a version, make a new one, hello-config-v2 next to v1.

81
00:04:34.654 --> 00:04:38.857
And immutable true makes sure nobody edits a version in place.

82
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:41.209
The moral of the story.

83
00:04:41.209 --> 00:04:45.378
Environment variables are the briefing, heard once, at the start.

84
00:04:45.378 --> 00:04:48.328
Mounted files are the board, updated in place.

85
00:04:48.328 --> 00:04:51.920
And only a new shift, a rollout restart, hears the news.

86
00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:53.094
One quick check.

87
00:04:53.094 --> 00:04:56.303
You change GREETING in hello-config, and apply it.

88
00:04:56.303 --> 00:04:59.063
What do the running hello-devops Pods show?

89
00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:02.839
The old greeting.

90
00:05:02.839 --> 00:05:04.660
They read it once, at start.

91
00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.367
Run a rollout restart, and the new Pods show the new one.
